#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# safekill — kill a process tree without the collateral-kill failure mode
# that `pkill -f` keeps producing in this project.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Two incidents on 2026-08-19, hours apart, same shape:
#   Incident A (wave B lane): `pkill -f` against a stale mech scratch-root
#   pattern killed a CONCURRENT, legitimate mech run as collateral (it
#   reported FATAL / 0 rows). First-recorded lesson: "never pkill -f a
#   pattern while another run of the same tool is live; kill by exact PID."
#   Incident B (the manager, same day): stopped a duplicate matrix run via
#   the task API — fine — then "cleaned up" with `pkill -f
#   run_sabotage_matrix`, which killed the OTHER, canonical run's live
#   child mid-row. The victim's log just stopped: no trailer, no FATAL,
#   because SIGTERM never reached the script's own accounting.
# THE SHARPENED LESSON (wave C's analysis): A COMMAND LINE IS NOT AN
# IDENTITY. Two legitimate concurrent invocations of the same tool are
# INDISTINGUISHABLE under any -f pattern — no amount of operator care
# fixes that — and the pattern can also match the caller's OWN wrapper
# shell (a `bash -c '...pattern text...'` matches itself; this is the root
# of this project's standing "never pgrep -f to poll liveness" rule,
# docs/dev/learnings.md section 6). The durable fixes, ordered by how
# little they ask of someone under pressure: kill the PROCESS GROUP of the
# shell you launched, or the recorded PID — never a name pattern. safekill
# makes the first the effortless default and keeps the second narrow and
# refuse-by-default on purpose.
#
# DESIGN CONSEQUENCES, each answering one incident:
#   - The PAVED ROAD is "I have a PID" (the common case: you started
#     something, you recorded its PID). safekill PID kills that PID's
#     process group AND its descendant tree (union of both — a
#     double-forked/daemonizing descendant can escape one mechanism but not
#     both: reparented-to-init but same pgid is caught by the group kill;
#     self-setsid'd into a new group but still a ppid-descendant is caught
#     by the tree walk). One call, no flags, safe by construction.
#   - The DANGEROUS ROAD is "I only have a pattern" (-f/--pattern). This
#     path is deliberately narrow: it lists candidates from raw /proc reads
#     ONLY — never `ps`/`pgrep`/any subprocess whose own argv could carry
#     the pattern text and match itself, which is exactly how a polling
#     wrapper self-matches under `pgrep -f` per the learnings.md rule.
#     Every candidate that is safekill's own PID or an ANCESTOR of the
#     caller (the whole chain up to PID 1, walked via /proc, unconditional,
#     no override flag: this is the "wrapper shell carries the pattern
#     text" failure reproduced exactly, and an override flag would just
#     re-open the hole) is dropped before anything else happens. If more
#     than one candidate remains, safekill REFUSES — this is incident
#     A/B's shape in miniature, two legitimate same-command-line processes
#     — unless --all is given explicitly. Refusal, not a guess.
#   - NEVER interactive. Agents cannot answer a confirmation prompt (they
#     are not attached to a terminal that will ever see one), so a design
#     that "asks before an ambiguous kill" would just hang or silently
#     skip the prompt depending on harness plumbing. Safety comes entirely
#     from refuse-by-default plus explicit flags, never a question.
#   - Every process actually signalled gets an audit line printed as it
#     happens: pid, pgid, start time, command — exactly the fields a
#     postmortem needs to answer "what did this kill and was it right",
#     which is precisely what incident B's victim log could NOT answer
#     (SIGTERM landed with zero accounting on the receiving end).
#   - Candidate scope narrower than the whole box, for the pattern path:
#     --under PID restricts candidates to PID's descendant tree (reliable
#     via /proc's ppid chain) and --cwd DIR restricts to processes whose
#     /proc/PID/cwd resolves under DIR (reliable via readlink, best-effort
#     for another user's processes whose cwd this UID cannot read — such
#     candidates are excluded, not treated as a hard error). Same-session
#     scoping was considered and NOT built as a default filter: incident
#     A/B's two collisions were both async/background invocations that may
#     not share a session with the caller, so defaulting to session-scope
#     would have hidden the exact case this tool exists to handle safely;
#     --under is the equivalent narrowing tool when the caller has
#     something to anchor on.
#   - Signal choice: TERM by default, escalate to KILL after a grace period
#     (--grace SECS, default 3) unless --term-only (never escalate) or
#     --kill-now/-K (skip TERM, go straight to KILL — for the genuinely
#     stuck). The escalation poll checks survivors in short increments
#     rather than sleeping the full grace, matching scripts/watchdog's
#     TERM->KILL shape.
#   - Exit codes are distinct on purpose so a caller can branch without
#     parsing text: 0 killed something, 1 nothing matched (not an error —
#     "already dead" is a fine outcome), 2 refused (ambiguous -f match,
#     needs --all), 125 usage error. See EXIT CODES below.
#
# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO (so a future reader does not propose
# rebuilding it): no confirmation prompts, ever — see above. No config
# file, no daemon/watch mode, no cross-machine kill, no privilege
# escalation (a candidate this UID cannot signal is reported and skipped,
# never sudo'd). No override for the self/ancestor exclusion — the one
# guard this tool has that the incidents show a human cannot reliably
# apply by hand, so it does not get a bypass flag. No "kill everything
# matching, no questions" default — --all exists and is one flag away, but
# the operator has to choose it, matching the incident record's actual
# lesson (operator care did not scale; refuse-by-default does).
#
# Usage:
#   scripts/safekill [OPTIONS] PID                # paved road: group+tree
#   scripts/safekill [OPTIONS] --pgid PGID         # exact group, no tree
#   scripts/safekill [OPTIONS] -f PATTERN          # narrow, refuse-by-default
#   scripts/safekill -h
#
# Options:
#   --pgid PGID     target an explicit process group id instead of a PID
#                   (kills exactly that group's current membership; no
#                   descendant-tree walk — PGID is already the whole ask)
#   -f, --pattern PAT
#                   match candidates by POSIX-ERE search against each
#                   process's full /proc/PID/cmdline (pkill -f's semantics:
#                   a search, not an anchored match). Dangerous path — see
#                   above. Self/ancestor candidates are dropped first,
#                   unconditionally; >1 remaining candidate REFUSES
#                   (exit 2) unless --all is given.
#   -a, --all       required to proceed when -f/--pattern matches more than
#                   one candidate after exclusions. Usage error with PID or
#                   --pgid targets (there is no ambiguity to widen there).
#   -n, --list      list matching candidates (pid/pgid/start/cmd) to stdout
#                   and exit WITHOUT signalling anything. Works with any
#                   target form; bypasses the ambiguity refusal (nothing is
#                   killed, so nothing needs refusing) — this is the safe
#                   way to preview a -f pattern before widening with --all.
#   --under PID     (with -f/--pattern only) restrict candidates to PID's
#                   descendant tree.
#   --cwd DIR       (with -f/--pattern only) restrict candidates to
#                   processes whose cwd resolves under DIR.
#   -s, --signal SIG
#                   initial signal, name or number (default TERM).
#   --grace SECS    seconds between the initial signal and KILL escalation
#                   (default 3).
#   --term-only     send the initial signal only; never escalate to KILL
#                   even if survivors remain after --grace.
#   -K, --kill-now  send KILL immediately — no initial signal, no grace.
#   -h, --help      this text.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0    at least one process was signalled.
#   1    no candidates matched (already dead, or the pattern/PID/PGID
#        found nothing) — not treated as an error.
#   2    refused: -f/--pattern matched more than one candidate after
#        exclusions and --all was not given.
#   125  usage error (bad arguments, target not found for --list, etc.).
#
# Mechanism notes: candidate discovery reads /proc/[0-9]*/stat,
# /proc/PID/cmdline and /proc/PID/cwd directly — no `ps`, no `pgrep`, no
# subprocess of any kind is spawned to find candidates, so there is no
# wrapper argv for a pattern to self-match against during discovery (only
# safekill's OWN argv can do that, and the ancestor-chain exclusion covers
# exactly that). Best-effort like scripts/watchdog's collect_stats: a
# process that exits mid-scan is silently skipped rather than treated as
# an error, and a candidate whose cwd this UID cannot read is excluded
# from --cwd matching rather than aborting the run.

set -u
echo "safekill: not yet implemented (requirements-only commit)" >&2
exit 125
